1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,076
A language model works with reusable choices called tokens: words or word-like symbols from a fixed vocabulary.

2
00:00:06,076 --> 00:00:10,674
We want similar Jewel shapes and colors to reuse the same tokens across many videos.

3
00:00:10,674 --> 00:00:13,520
This is not an attempt to compress a finished video.

4
00:00:13,520 --> 00:00:19,760
The goal is to give a future generator a stable set of physical words it can speak into an empty spacetime volume.

5
00:00:20,460 --> 00:00:25,632
The same tilted ellipsoid can be written with several different rotation-number combinations.

6
00:00:25,632 --> 00:00:28,301
The picture does not change, but the numbers do.

7
00:00:28,301 --> 00:00:33,195
A learner would mistake those spellings for different shapes and waste vocabulary space.

8
00:00:33,195 --> 00:00:37,866
Before creating tokens, we convert every Jewel to one consistent numerical spelling.

9
00:00:37,866 --> 00:00:43,260
In mathematics this removes a gauge ambiguity; in plain language, it removes meaningless aliases.

10
00:00:43,960 --> 00:00:49,089
We store each shape as a symmetric three-by-three table, which needs six unique numbers.

11
00:00:49,089 --> 00:00:55,558
We also take a matrix logarithm, a standard transformation that makes large and small scales easier to compare.

12
00:00:55,558 --> 00:01:01,094
The important result is simple: identical visible shapes now receive identical stored features.

13
00:01:01,094 --> 00:01:05,640
The later renderer can convert that stable spelling back into widths and tilt.

14
00:01:06,340 --> 00:01:11,880
Our first attempt bundled layout, shape, color, and color change into one enormous choice.

15
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:16,558
That was like printing every possible sentence as a single dictionary entry.

16
00:01:16,558 --> 00:01:17,174
It failed.

17
00:01:17,174 --> 00:01:21,052
The stronger design gives each role its own list of prototypes.

18
00:01:21,052 --> 00:01:24,437
A prototype is simply a learned representative example.

19
00:01:24,437 --> 00:01:29,300
The model can then combine a familiar shape with a familiar color in a new way.

20
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,200
Four lists of one thousand twenty-four choices can describe far more combinations than one list of the same size.

21
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:40,974
We do not build a table containing every combination; we compose the roles when needed.

22
00:01:40,974 --> 00:01:47,942
For an individual Jewel, position remains continuous while shape, color, and color change come from their learned vocabularies.

23
00:01:47,942 --> 00:01:52,880
This gives the speaker reusable parts without forcing every result to copy a stored whole.

24
00:01:53,580 --> 00:01:58,585
The learner uses coarse boxes to ask which tokens are common in each neighborhood.

25
00:01:58,585 --> 00:02:02,796
Those boxes are addresses for routing information, like postal codes.

26
00:02:02,796 --> 00:02:08,412
They are not the final Jewel positions, just as a postal code is not a chair inside a house.

27
00:02:08,412 --> 00:02:12,379
The model still emits a continuous center that can land anywhere.

28
00:02:12,379 --> 00:02:15,980
Discrete routing and irregular geometry can safely coexist.

29
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:21,857
We tested whether replacing continuous features with vocabulary choices destroyed the field.

30
00:02:21,857 --> 00:02:30,862
The reconstructed images reached about twenty-two point nine decibels of peak signal-to-noise ratio, a standard image-similarity measure where higher is better.

31
00:02:30,862 --> 00:02:34,913
Spacetime tilt was preserved, and no centers locked to the routing grid.

32
00:02:34,913 --> 00:02:37,277
This does not prove promptable generation.

33
00:02:37,277 --> 00:02:41,160
It shows the physical vocabulary is usable enough for the next level.
